WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.083
Scenario 2 walkthrough: Code Generation with Claude Code.

2
00:00:04.083 --> 00:00:13.385
A team that shares Claude Code needs shared configuration, so this scenario asks where each instruction should live and when to plan before editing.

3
00:00:14.785 --> 00:00:23.218
Scenario two is a development team using Claude Code every day for generating code, refactoring, debugging and writing documentation.

4
00:00:23.218 --> 00:00:33.045
They have custom slash commands and skills, CLAUDE.md files at several levels, and they have to decide when to plan first and when to just make the change.

5
00:00:33.045 --> 00:00:37.356
Most questions come from domain three, with some context management.

6
00:00:38.756 --> 00:00:51.921
This scenario leans on the CLAUDE.md hierarchy and path-scoped rules, custom commands and skills, the choice between plan mode and direct execution, and iterative refinement with examples and tests.

7
00:00:51.921 --> 00:00:59.833
From domain five, it adds managing context while exploring a large codebase, for example with subagents and compaction.

8
00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:02.446
A typical question.

9
00:01:02.446 --> 00:01:08.444
A new engineer joins and their Claude Code ignores the testing conventions everyone else gets.

10
00:01:08.444 --> 00:01:16.804
The cause is that the conventions were written in the lead's user level CLAUDE dot md, which is personal and not in the repository.

11
00:01:16.804 --> 00:01:22.993
The fix is to move them into the project file or the project rules folder so everyone loads them.

12
00:01:24.393 --> 00:01:26.174
Now the plan mode decision.

13
00:01:26.174 --> 00:01:34.020
A change that spans many files, affects architecture or has more than one reasonable approach deserves plan mode first.

14
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.922
A small, clear, well scoped change does not.

15
00:01:36.922 --> 00:01:43.714
When the work is mostly discovery, an Explore subagent keeps the noisy output out of your main context.

16
00:01:43.714 --> 00:01:46.153
Then you execute the plan you agreed.

17
00:01:47.553 --> 00:01:48.704
Common traps here.

18
00:01:48.704 --> 00:01:54.011
Copying the same rule into many directory files when one path scoped rule would do.

19
00:01:54.011 --> 00:01:58.742
Running a noisy skill in the main session rather than forking its context.

20
00:01:58.742 --> 00:02:00.724
Using plan mode for everything.

21
00:02:00.724 --> 00:02:08.012
And describing desired behaviour in prose when a concrete example or a failing test would steer Claude far better.

22
00:02:09.412 --> 00:02:11.489
For scenario two, remember this.

23
00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:16.551
Shared rules belong in project level files, not in anyone's personal settings.

24
00:02:16.551 --> 00:02:20.445
Use path scoped rules for conventions that span directories.

25
00:02:20.445 --> 00:02:24.923
Use skills for workflows you call on demand, and fork the noisy ones.

26
00:02:24.923 --> 00:02:30.959
And plan before large or ambiguous changes, while small clear fixes go straight to execution.

